AB  - In the first chapter of the thesis, we examine the impact of protected bike lanes by studying a quasi-experiment in which new protected bike lanes were installed on Burrard Bridge in Vancouver, Canada, in 2009. Using detailed panel data and two other locations in Vancouver as controls in a difference-in-differences (DiD) framework, we find that the new lanes significantly increase bicycle ridership by about 23%, accompanied by over a 7.3% reduction in road vehicle traffic. Back-of-the-envelope calculations suggest that the elasticity of vehicle travel with respect to biking is approximately -0.3. To the best of our knowledge, these findings provide the first rigorous empirical estimates regarding the causal relationships between safe bike infrastructure, bike ridership, and vehicle traffic.&nbsp;
