AB  - In this paper, I study the agricultural market outcomes of connecting over 150,000 isolated villages to the wider Indian economy. Utilising India’s $40 billion rural roads program, I find that improving connection to previously isolated areas results in an increase in prices received by farmers. I find evidence that this is a result of increased bargaining power, as farmers can now travel farther and interact with more markets. Higher prices generated by connection causes increased agricultural output, compounding the welfare gains of price rises. These changes are robust to shifts in the composition of the workforce. In more rural areas agriculture’s share of the workforce increases, while decreasing in less rural areas.
