AB  - This research examines the impact of road construction on rural labor force outcomes in adeveloping country. A new road built in the Republic of Palau links formerly inaccessible ruralareas to more urban wage sector employment. We use two censuses conducted five yearsapart which perfectly bracket the road construction period. The data allow us to identifyhouseholds that moved in the intervening five year period, thereby correcting anyendogenous movement attributable to the road construction. Utilizing a difference-indifference regression strategy and matched panel data, we find that households impacted bythe new road construction tend to increase their wage sector employment, decrease theirself-employment in agriculture, decrease the number of international migrants sent abroadand increase their ownership of automobiles. The findings also show that inequalitydecreases both within and between regions. The impact of road construction on averagehousehold wages and income is negligible
