TY  - Thesis
TI  - The Effects of Labor Market Integration: Evidence from Germany’s High-Speed Rail
AU  - Liu, Jianan
JO  - Other
VL  - 
IS  - 
PY  - 2022
DO  - 
AB  - My dissertation studies labor market structure and evaluates effects of policies relating to transportation improvements and immigration enforcement in different countries including Germany, the Untied States, and China. Specifically, I examine thenature and extent of labor market frictions, explore the causes and consequences, as well as study the role in policy evaluations.In the first chapter, I investigate one potential source of labor market frictions from limited labor mobility by examining how integrating labor markets through improved transportation infrastructure affects both wages and the allocation of workersacross establishments. I take advantage of the expansion of the High-Speed Rail in Germany, which connected medium-sized districts located on existing rail lines, providing a natural experiment to study the effects of labor market integration. Using administrative panel data on establishments and workers linked to their employers, I estimate difference-in-differences and event-study models that compare newly connected districts to matched controls that were never connected. In theory, policies that improve labor mobility might raise wages both by facilitating more productive matches of workers to firms and by reducing the monopsony power that employers have on workers. I find evidence of increased labor mobility for workers in treated districts, especially those that are more likely to commute. Worker wages increase significantly and I find that both reduced monopsony power and better match quality are possible mechanisms. I also test for establishment adjustments including entry, exit, size, and wage that are predicted by each mechanism. This study shows evidence of reduced labor market frictions from improved transportation. It also sheds light on the importance of policies that enhance workers’ ability to switch employers.In the second chapter, I explore the effects of LAWA on labor markets based on evidence that LAWA has significantly reduced the population of Hispanic noncitizens in Arizona. Specifically, I focus on the composition of the labor force and industry heterogeneity. I first show that a synthetic control has similar concurrent economic trends with Arizona, and verify that changes in employee composition are due to the replacement of Hispanic noncitizen workers by other subgroups and not by a change in overall employment. In response to LAWA, firms tend to reduce both the new hire rate and the separation rate. Several robustness checks are conducted to test the accuracy of the estimates and several mechanisms are considered that may drive the results.In the third chapter, we study the structure of labor markets and the effects on wages in China. A growing literature has emphasized the existence of monopsony power stemming from employer concentration within local labor markets, which deviates from the conventional view of labor markets as perfectly competitive. We use firm-level data from the Chinese Annual Survey of Industrial Firms to analyze how employer concentration affects wage behavior. We first verify that local employment concentration measured by the Herfindahl-Hirschman Index of firm employment decreases between 1998 and 2013, in contrast to the increasing trend found in developed countries. Then with OLS and IV models, we show a negative relationship between labor market concentration and wages both the at the market level and at the firm level, indicating theexistence of imperfect labor market competition in China.
UR  - https://escholarship.org/uc/item/6xb6926f
ER  - 

TY  - Thesis
TI  - Three Essays in Development Economics
AU  - Demie, Muse Gadisa
JO  - Other
VL  - 
IS  - 
PY  - 2018
DO  - 10.26190/unsworks/20809
AB  - This dissertation consists of three independent essays in development economics. The first chapter is “Lineage, Cereals and Gender Roles: Evidence from Sub-Saharan Africa.” This chapter examines the historical origins of contemporary differences in norms and beliefs about the role of women in society within Africa. I test the hypothesis that traditional cultivation of different crop types influenced the historical gender division of labor and the evolution and persistence of gender norms. Anthropological accounts suggest that societies that have relied historically on the cultivation of cereals (as opposed to root or tuber crops) for their diet have developed a specialization of production where women tended to work within the home, and this, in turn, gave rise to less equality in gender roles. Using ethnicity to link individuals today to their ancestors’ agricultural practices and exploiting variation in the cultivation of different crops across ethnic groups, I find that, consistent with existing hypotheses, women from ethnicities that derived their subsistence from the cultivation of cereals in the pre-colonial era are today less likely to be in the labor force, have higher levels of fertility, and are less likely to participate in household decisions. In addition, women belonging to these groups are more likely to have attitudes favoring gender inequality and domestic violence. To address causality, I use land suitability for cereals relative to suitability for roots or tubers as an instrument for the actual cultivation of cereal grains in the pre-colonial period. Examining the causal mechanisms, I show that cultural norms and beliefs, which are transmitted over generations and through the marriage market represent important channels explaining long-run persistence. The second chapter is “The Persistent Effects of Transportation Infrastructure. vi vii Evidence from Colonial Railroads in Tanzania.” In this chapter, I exploit the construction and eventual demise of the colonial railroads in Tanzania to study the persistent impact of investments in transportation infrastructure on urban population growth and economic development. Using geo-referenced data at a very fine spatial level, I find a strong positive long-term effect of railroad connectivity on urbanization and local development, proxied by night-time lights. To address non-random line placements, I propose an instrumental variable, propensity score matching, and placebo specifications. The instrument identifies exogenous variation in railroad connectivity by constructing the least cost path (LCP) between major rail nodes. I find no effect of ’placebo’ railroad lines that were planned but were never actually constructed. I provide evidence in support of human capital accumulation being the potential channelof persistence. First, I show using household-level survey data, that individuals living in railway-connected areas are more educated, more literate, and more likely to work in modern higher paying occupations. Second, I find that colonial railroad had a significant impact on European and Asian settlement during the colonial era. Since both groups were relatively skilled, investments in physical capital thus attracted human capital. Third, I find evidence that colonial railroads had also influenced the location choice of Christian missions, the primary providers of schooling and medical services in colonial Tanzania. Finally, I show that the location of colonial railroad affected the placement decisions of subsequent colonial human capital public infrastructures such as schools and hospitals. The third chapter is “Income Shocks and Civil Conflict in Africa.” In this chapter, I study the impact of plausibly exogenous local income variations on civil conflict. Ifirst present a model that shows how variation in local economic returns in the laborintensive agricultural sector affect appropriable rents and the opportunity costs of fighting. The theory predicts that rainfall shocks -a major source of income variation for rural households- affect conflicts through their effect on agricultural income. I test these predictions using panel data on conflict events covering all sub-Saharan African countries at a spatial resolution of 0.5 × 0.5 degree latitude and longitude over the 1997-2016 period. Exploiting within grid cell-year variation in rainfall and in crop growing season, and spatial variation in crop cover to identify local exogenous viii changes in income, I show that the incidence, magnitude, and frequency of droughts occurring during the growing season of the local dominant crop have a robust positiveeffect on civil conflict. This effect is highest in cells where agricultural cropland constitutes the largest share of land cover. I explore two additional possible channels through which rainfall variability in general and droughts in particular, might induce conflict: ethnic cleavages and competition over scarce natural resources.
UR  - https://developmentevidence.3ieimpact.org/search-result-details/impact-evaluation-repository/three-essays-in-development-economics/30059
