AB  - In “VancUber,” we investigate the long-run effect of ride-hailing on public transit ridership, traffic congestion, and traffic fatalities. We estimate the long-run effect of ride-hailing by exploiting British Columbia’s use of a pre-existing regulation in 2013 to ban ride-hailing from Vancouver and using the synthetic control method to construct a counterfactual Vancouver. We do not find a statistically significant effect of ride-hailing on our outcomes. Our results for fatalities are imprecise, but our confidence intervals for the effect on transit ridership and congestion provide bounds on the likely effect that are smaller in magnitude than many existing estimates
