AB  - The 21st century has seen rapid innovation in digital platforms for transportation. Starting in 2005 with the development of the General Transit Feed Specification (GTFS), transit agencies around the world have released open-source information on their routes and vehicles. This has allowed mapping applications such as Google Maps to help users plan transit trips with accurate, up-to-date information. With the development of modern smartphones, beginning with the first iPhone in 2007, users could have transit information anywhere, anytime through one of many mapping applications connected to GTFS feeds. Once smartphones became ubiquitous, new platforms emerged to allow users to summon a vehicle through their phones. Ride-hailing platforms Uber and Lyft both launched in San Francisco in 2010 and have emerged as major competitors to the taxi industry by connecting drivers with passengers through digital platforms. Others followed, with entrants to the space creating digital platforms for users to locate and rent other vehicles such as bicycles and scooters, as well as vans and larger vehicles offering shared service (i.e. “microtransit”). We examine whether the integration of various transportation modes within an aggregator app can enhance the adoption of ride-hailing as a first- and last-mile solution for transit. A popular mapping app introduced a multimodal route planning feature on November 27, 2018, incorporating bus connections in select cities in the United States and rail connections supported universally. We assess the change in multimodal behavior by measuring the shift in the proportion of ride-hailing trips starting or ending at transit stops. To see if ride-hailing trips start or end at transit stops, we utilize datasets consisting of 110,214 ride-hailing trips from the app and the locations of 891,325 transit stops from publicly available GTFS in the United States, and compare the start and end locations of the ride-hailing trips with transit stops using a k-nearest-neighbor search algorithm (Crookston and Finley, 2008).
UR  - https://summit.sfu.ca/_flysystem/fedora/2024-05/etd23029.pdf
ER  - 

TY  - Thesis
TI  - Traffic Improvement or Disruption: Ride-hailing in London
AU  - Junxiao, Ma
JO  - Other
VL  - 
IS  - 
PY  - 2021
DO  - NO DOI
AB  - This dissertation includes three essays exploring topics related to Urban Economics. Using advanced empirical methodologies, I examine the effects of traffic pricing policies and rider-hailing services on traffic and air quality. Chapter 1 investigates the traffic and environmental impact of traffic pricing policy. As a new solution to traffic congestion, road pricing has been applied in several European and U.S. cities.However, road pricing, especially cordon charge, has not received much attention to assessing its economic impact from economists. This paper fills the gap by estimating the heterogeneous effect of the London congestion charging scheme (CCZ) on the traffic by vehicles with different pollution intensities and ambient air quality within the Greater London Area. Employing a difference in difference method, this paper finds evidence of alleviated traffic and reduction of air pollution following the CCZ order. The implementation of such a policy effectively reduces the traffic flows from less-polluted vehicles, like private cars by roughly 16% inside central London. However, about 25% more trips by polluting vehicles (light and heavy goods vehicles) are induced from this traffic charge. The environmental impact of the congestion charge also varies across different air pollutants. About 28% and 18% drops in NO and NO2 emissions are detected following CCZ order. PM2:5 reduces slightly by 6% after the charge. However, other air pollutants, such as O3 and CO experience no significant changes under the charge.Chapter 2 examines the effects of the air pollution charge on traffic by vehicle types. While there exists a growing branch of literature evaluating traffic pricing, especially congestion charge, economists have not extended their research assessing another major traffic pricing policy: air pollution charge. Compared to the congestion charge, air pollution pricing specifically targets highly polluting vehicles, such as heavy trucks. By covering both urban and rural areas with an all-time charge, transportation agencies consider it as a more advanced and effective solution to congestion. However, due to a poor understanding of its impact on road traffic, they are having difficulties assessing its economic impact. This paper bridge this gap by estimating the heterogeneous effect of the Low Emission Zone (LEZ) on highway traffic volume in the Greater London Area (GLA). The introduction of LEZ leads to about an 11% drop in overall traffic. However, the response to air pollution charges varies across different vehicle groups. Following the enactment of LEZ, traffic by light goods vehicle (LGV) decreases by roughly 14% while traffic of heavy good vehicle (HGV) increases by 18%. My results suggest the effectiveness of cordon charge to reduce overall traffic flow. However, the overall traffic drop marks important heterogeneity across vehicle types with potential substitution from LGV to HGV to avoid multiple charges.Chapter 3 studies the impact of ride-hailing services on the traffic of different automobiles. Effective regulation of transportation network agencies (TNC) has long been a political and technical challenge due to a lack of deep understanding of the impact of ride-hailing services on local traffic. This paper studies the effect of such a service on traffic using a natural experiment created by the abrupt exit of Uber from London. Adopting the difference-in-difference approach with granular traffic data, my study finds an overall drop of 13.2% in average traffic volume and an increase of 21.2% in average traffic speed following the suspension order of Uber in London. For other transit modes, the evidence in support of a rise of 13.7% in public traffic and 19.6% in private-hire traffic is found after the temporary exit of Uber. Together these results suggest that both private-hire vehicles, such as taxis and public transit serve as substitutes for the ride-hailing services. More importantly, my study also implies that the ride-induction effect dominates the ride-sharing effect of ride-hailing services. The three natural experiment studies overlap either geographically or temporally. Chapter 1 focuses on the CCZ implementation between 2000 and 2007 in central London area. Chapter 2 studies the LEZ during the period of 2000-2012 that covers the Greater London Area, including CCZ. The enactment of CCZ is likely to motivate the drivers to shift their travels from inside the charging zone to the boundary zone to avoid the charge. Such a spillover effect tends to positively bias the traffic impact estimation of LEZ. The discussion of the interaction and solution to the confounding effects are carefully discussed in the Robustness Check Section of Chapter 2. Chapter 3 investigates the effect of temporary departure of Uber between 2017 and 2018 by estimating the traffic volume variation in central London Area. Since the LEZ charge only targets commercial vehicles, ride-hailing vehicles will not be affected. However, Uber drivers have to pay the daily congestion charge inside the city center. This traffic charge could suppress the supply of Uber drivers before the Uber ban order and traffic of other transit mode during the suspension period. However, as shown in the Section of empirical results in Chapter 3, traffic in London generally increase after the ban ruling.
UR  - https://ucalgary.scholaris.ca/items/25437cd9-9ea8-484b-9919-d13b15927a49
