AB  - The existence of productivity externalities of transport improvements has seen growing attention in the last decade. These benefits are additional to benefits captured in the standard cost-benefit analysis. The theoretical foundation of such effects is a combination of changes in city size-through larger labor markets-and a positive relationship between city size and productivity. Most empirical contributions study this last relationship, while little attention has been given to effects of larger labor markets. This paper investigates larger labor market effect, using commuting as an indicator. The effects are studied using the Eiksund Connection in Norway, which is a sub-sea tunnel replacing a ferry service. Using the synthetic control method, the paper shows commuting effects for only the municipalities with the largest accessibility improvement and the strongest need for specialized skills; the effects for the other municipalities are insignificant. Hence, the agglomeration benefits are likely to be small.
