AB  - This series of essays explores different ways in which community factors are connected to labor market outcomes in the United States. The first essay assesses the causal impact of public transportation improvements on labor market outcomes. The other two essays look at the correlation between the socioeconomic mobility, as measured by Chetty and Hendren (2018), and other measures of time-use and skill. Chapter 2 examines the impact of public transportation investments on labor market outcomes, including employment and job-to-job switching. By comparing places that pass and fail ballot initiatives on transit funding, I examine the impact of transportation improvements. I do not find evidence that increased transit funding leads to higher job-to-job switching rates or hiring rates. However, I do find that employment and population increase. Chapter 3, written with Sulagna Mookerjee and David Slichter, examines the impact of place on time-use. We regress various measures of family time-use with measures of intergenerational mobility, as measured by Chetty and Hendren (2018). We find that parents living in counties with higher mobility spend more time interacting with their children and at work. They also spend less time doing community activities and in education. Chapter 4, written with David Slichter, examines the relationship between community quality and the skills that workers growing up in these communities develop. Are quality communities "biased" towards teaching certain types of skills, such the types of skills that are used by teachers or scientists? We find that community quality is most associated with cognitive skills, typically used by management and other high-earning occupations.
